From 6a428aa1220b6a40ad12cd1c39c0c21efa8b08ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:19:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] goal loop: interrupt provenance, a first-class blocked
 verdict, and a resume that re-arms
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An autonomous goal loop stalled at turn 3 of 400 and stayed stalled. Four
defects, none of them the one the symptom pointed at.

MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. The judge returned ZERO `pause` verdicts in 244 logged
calls (157 continue / 68 defer / 14 wait / 5 done) -- its verdict contract has
no `pause` at all, so no mid-flight pause can ever have come from a judge
verdict. The stalled row read paused_reason="user-interrupted (Ctrl+C)" while
last_verdict="continue". The discriminator against a healthy session is not
goal text: the stall happened moments after a continuation was injected, and
nobody touched the keyboard.  [measured]

1. A MACHINE INTERRUPT IS NOT THE HUMAN'S SIGNAL.
   `_last_turn_interrupted` was True for every interrupt, including a
   provider-stall circuit breaker and a stream abort on a background thread.
   Both pause paths labelled those "user-interrupted (Ctrl+C)" -- the one
   class an auto-resume supervisor is forbidden to touch -- so a transient
   became a permanent silent stall with 397 turns unspent. Interrupts now
   carry PROVENANCE: only the four seams a person drives (Ctrl+C, ESC, a
   message typed mid-turn, a voice interjection) set it, and only those may
   pause. A machine interrupt keeps the goal ACTIVE, re-arms the
   continuation, consumes no turn, and does not spend a judge call on an
   "Operation interrupted" stub. Signal handlers stay machine-provenance
   deliberately.

2. THE JUDGE WAS INSTRUCTED TO REPORT "BLOCKED" AS DONE.
   The system prompt said: "The response explains the goal is unachievable /
   blocked / needs user input (treat this as DONE with reason describing the
   block)". All five measured `done` verdicts are that shape. That is the
   retired-at-54-of-400 failure: a multi-part goal closed under a tick over
   work that had not happened. The instruction is gone; breadth is now
   explicitly named as never a reason to stop; and `blocked` is a first-class
   verdict that PAUSES with the human decision named and the remaining budget
   stated, carrying a marker no machine may auto-resume.

   A deterministic net sits UNDER the prompt and reclassifies a `done` whose
   own reason describes a block, because a language model is not a parser.
   The net is built from the five verbatim verdicts, not from invented ones:
   the exact phrasings are what it has to catch.

3. `/goal resume` DID NOT RESUME.
   resume() only flipped the status. Continuations are enqueued by a POST-TURN
   hook and a slash command never runs a turn, so the supervisor found the
   paused goal, proved the pane idle, sent `/goal resume` -- and the goal sat
   active and idle. Resume now re-arms the loop, deferring to any queued human
   message.

4. THE SUPERVISOR GAINS THE STALL CLASS IT WAS BLIND TO.
   Every check was keyed on status == "paused", so an `active` goal that had
   silently dropped its continuation -- the exact shape of this stall -- was
   invisible. stalled_active_goals() catches active + turns left + no barrier
   + no turn for --stall-seconds (20 min default). Every uncertainty resolves
   to NOT a candidate: a parked goal, a spent budget and a missing
   last_turn_at are all left alone. The idle proof (turn lease + busy markers
   + byte-identical pane) and the rate limit apply unchanged to both classes,
   so it still cannot type into a live turn.

84 new or updated tests, including a regression test built from the stalled
session's verbatim judge verdict asserting it does NOT pause. Nothing in this
patch reaches a running process; it takes effect at the next relaunch.

BASE: the tree left by patch 0006 (see BASELINE.md).

PUBLISHED FORM. The regression corpus in
tests/hermes_cli/test_goal_judge_never_stops_midflight.py is five production
judge verdicts, and three of them named a third-party platform and a blocked
commercial submission. Paraphrasing them would destroy their value -- the
exact phrasings ARE the evidence the classifier has to catch -- so they are
SUBSTITUTED with structurally equivalent synthetic strings: the same shape,
the same trigger words ("blocked pending", "still pending", "upload-ready",
"login from the owner"), invented subject matter, and an invented platform
name. The two that carried no third-party subject are verbatim. The
classifier's own patterns are unchanged, and the test still passes, which is
the check that the substitution preserved what mattered. Also substituted:
one session id, in six places; an internal pane reference; and nothing else.

