+"""The judge may stop a goal for three reasons, and no others [bd-dre-df78a].
+
+⛔ THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS FOR. Andre, 2026-09-30: session 20260819_144700_aaaaaa
+(the peer pane) kept stalling at turn 3 of 400. A judge that cannot tell "not finished
+yet" from "should stop" costs hours of idle GPU.
+
+Two defects, in opposite directions, both measured on this box:
+
+1. **Mid-flight progress must never stop a goal.** The verdicts logged for that
+   session were ``continue``; the *pause* came from the CLI's interrupt path
+   (see ``tests/cli/test_cli_goal_interrupt.py``). The judge side of the
+   contract still has to hold: a broad, unfinished, multi-part goal is the
+   NORMAL mid-flight state and the verdict for it is ``continue``.
+
+2. **"Blocked" must never be reported as "done".** ``JUDGE_SYSTEM_PROMPT`` used
+   to instruct exactly that — *"The response explains the goal is unachievable /
+   blocked / needs user input (treat this as DONE…)"* — and all five ``done``
+   verdicts in ~/.hermes/profiles/architect/logs were that shape. That is the
+   ``done``-at-54/400 failure: a multi-part goal retired with a "✓ Goal
+   achieved" over work that had not happened.
+
